      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "56088e33-eba0-5528-a953-a73a88501cfb",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Arthur Rudolph, Wernher von Braun\n- Organizations: Office of Strategic Services, Joint Intelligence Objectives Agency, United States Government\n- Programs: Operation Paperclip\n- Topics: U.S. space program, V-2 rocket team, post-World War II recruitment\n\nFacts:\n- At the end of World War II, Operation Paperclip was a secret program of the Joint Intelligence Objectives Agency to recruit more than 1,600 German scientists, engineers, and technicians from post-Nazi Germany for U.S. government employment.\n- At the end of World War II, Operation Paperclip recruited figures such as Wernher von Braun and his V-2 rocket team, and many recruits were members of the Nazi Party while some were Nazi Party leaders.\n- After World War II, the United States Government's Office of Strategic Services brought Arthur Rudolph to the United States as part of the clandestine Operation Paperclip.\n- After World War II, Arthur Rudolph became one of the main developers of the U.S. space program.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "bd76bc1d-846c-5dbd-afca-6aa0b131bc07",
        "text": "Page: Arthur Rudolph. Sentence: After the war, the United States Government's Office of Strategic Services (OSS) brought him to the U.S. as part of the clandestine Operation Paperclip, where he became one of the main developers of the U.S. space program.\nPage: Operation Paperclip. Sentence: Operation Paperclip was a secret program of the Joint Intelligence Objectives Agency (JIOA) in which more than 1,600 German scientists, engineers, and technicians, such as Wernher von Braun and his V-2 rocket team, were recruited in post-Nazi Germany and taken to the U.S. for government employment, at the end of World War II; many were members and some were leaders of the Nazi Party.",
